<body>
<div class=3D"root">

<h1>HNSW - Hierarchical Navigable Small World</h1>
<p class=3D"sub">How vector databases find nearest neighbors in millisecond=
s across 10M+ vectors</p>

<!-- INTRO -->
<div class=3D"card card-accent">
  <p><span class=3D"highlight">HNSW</span> is a graph-based algorithm for A=
pproximate Nearest Neighbor (ANN) search. Given a large collection of high-=
dimensional vectors and a query vector, it finds the K most similar vectors=
 in <span class=3D"highlight">milliseconds</span> - without scanning the en=
tire dataset.</p>
  <p style=3D"margin-top:10px">It works by building a <strong style=3D"colo=
r:#cdd9e5">multi-layer graph</strong> where upper layers act as express rou=
tes across the entire vector space, and the bottom layer holds every vector=
 with dense local connections. A query starts at the top, navigates down la=
yer by layer, and arrives at the exact neighbourhood of the answer - visiti=
ng only a tiny fraction of the index.</p>
  <p style=3D"margin-top:10px">Published by Malkov &amp; Yashunin in 2018, =
it became the default ANN index for almost every vector database because it=
 combines <span class=3D"highlight-teal">high recall</span>, <span class=3D=
"highlight-teal">low latency</span>, and <span class=3D"highlight-teal">dyn=
amic inserts</span> without needing a training or clustering phase.</p>
</div>

<div class=3D"card">
  <h3>Where HNSW is used</h3>
  <p style=3D"margin-top:0.4rem">Used as the default ANN index in <span cla=
ss=3D"highlight">Pinecone</span>, <span class=3D"highlight">Weaviate</span>=
, <span class=3D"highlight">Qdrant</span>, <span class=3D"highlight">pgvect=
or</span>, <span class=3D"highlight">FAISS</span>, <span class=3D"highlight=
">Chroma</span>, and <span class=3D"highlight">Milvus</span>.</p>
</div>

